<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=dome">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dome</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A dome is an architectural element resembling the hollow upper half of a sphere; the earliest found may be four dwellings of mammoth tusks and bones at Mezhirich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, dated between 19,280 and 11,700 BCE. Corbelled stone domes appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, and domes were built in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and in Persian, Hellenistic, Roman and Chinese architecture. They reached monumental size in the Roman Imperial period, whose largest dome is the Pantheon, and were later common in Byzantine, Islamic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and Ottoman architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome.jpg" alt="Dome" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerda Arendt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachen_Cathedral,_octogon_towards_cupola_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>19,280–11,700 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mezhirich, Ukraine</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>When the first dome was built is not known; the mammoth-bone dwellings of Mezhirich may be the earliest. Corbelled stone domes appear in the Neolithic Near East.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=dome">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
